# tests/test_kill_plateau.bd - T13: THE SUSTAINED PLATEAU.
# Fourth and last test of the KILL-TRIGGER block (T10 duration, T11 burst
# size, T12 reset rule, T13 LEVEL).
#
# =====================================================================
# ONE QUESTION
# =====================================================================
# Is the kill threshold literally the CEILING (100%), or will a load held
# steadily BELOW the ceiling - 95, 90, 82, 70, 60, 50 - also kill if it is
# sustained long enough?
#
# =====================================================================
# WHY NO PRIOR TEST ANSWERED THIS
# =====================================================================
# Every "survived at cpu=80" result in this project came out of a CLOSED
# THROTTLE LOOP: work, then `while get_cpu() > threshold: sleep(1)`. Two
# fatal properties of that shape:
#   1. It regulates whatever you put inside it, so it can essentially
#      never crash - which is exactly why all four prior hammering tests
#      (test_decode_pacing, test_token_dispatch, test_dispatch_after_
#      imports) survived 500-1400s without ever reproducing anything.
#   2. It samples get_cpu() at ONE phase of the cycle - the instant the
#      throttle returned, i.e. the one moment per cycle the controller
#      had just guaranteed the number was low. test_decode_pacing PART B
#      sat at a LOGGED 80 for 1442 seconds, but its true instantaneous
#      load certainly hit 100 inside every burst. That run is evidence
#      about a controller, not about a load level.
#
# This is the first OPEN-LOOP design in the corpus. There is no get_cpu()
# anywhere in the control path. The load level is set purely by
# arithmetic and sleep(), and get_cpu() is read twice per block as a
# passive witness that cannot steer anything.
#
# =====================================================================
# HOW THE PLATEAU IS BUILT
# =====================================================================
# A CELL = a fixed block of 10 unrolled `a = a + 1` lines (the same unit
# T02's ruler is calibrated in), then one sleep(S). With spu seconds per
# executed line, a cell costs ~14 lines of busy time (10 units + the
# while-condition + the counter + the sleep line + the per-block
# instrument amortised) and then idles for S seconds. So:
#
#     duty = busy / (busy + S),   busy = 14 * spu
#     S    = busy * (100 - L) / L
#
# The script computes S ITSELF from the requested level and CPU tier.
# Nothing is hard-coded to base tier and there is no ladder of magic
# millisecond constants to mis-transcribe. Ask for 82, get 82.
#
# A BLOCK = 4 cells (40 units, 4 sleeps) + one instrument line. The
# plateau runs blocks until `hold` seconds have elapsed (default 60 =
# TWELVE times the documented 5-second kill window), not until a fixed
# block count - so the run takes the same wall-clock time at every rung
# and on every CPU tier.
#
# =====================================================================
# THE POSITIVE CONTROL - RUN THIS ARM FIRST, ALWAYS
# =====================================================================
#     test_kill_plateau 100
# sets S = 0 and takes a separate loop with NO sleep line at all in the
# cell: a genuinely unpaced plateau at duty=100 by construction.
#
# IT MUST DIE, within ~5 seconds, i.e. inside blk 1 or 2 (a block is ~5.6s
# of solid execution at base tier). If it does not, THIS SCRIPT HAS NO
# POWER TO KILL ANYTHING and every "survived" line from the ladder below
# is worthless - exactly the trap all four prior hammering tests fell
# into. Stop the ladder and report that instead: "the engine did not kill
# a 60-second totally unpaced burn loop" would be the largest single
# finding of this whole investigation.
#
# =====================================================================
# PREDICTIONS - STATED IN ADVANCE
# =====================================================================
# PRIMARY (threshold IS the ceiling). Every paced rung, including 95,
#   prints its SURVIVED line. Only lvl=100 dies. Mechanism this supports:
#   get_cpu() is a ~1-second sliding count of ticks executed / ticks
#   available (this is what a rock-steady idle reading of exactly 10.0 on
#   a 10-ticks/sec tier means - one executed line per second), and the
#   killer wants that window FULL for 5 consecutive seconds. Any duty
#   below 100 at any grain finer than 5 seconds can then never kill,
#   no matter how long it is sustained.
#
# SECONDARY (threshold is a lower sustained X). There is a rung that dies
#   and a rung one step above it that survives all the way to `hold`.
#   Deaths land in the first 1-2 blocks (the plateau establishes within a
#   block; if it were going to kill, it kills immediately - a death at
#   blk 9 of 10 means something ACCUMULATES and is a different finding
#   again, see below). X is then read straight off the surviving
#   neighbour's own printed duty= column, not off the requested level.
#
# SLOW ACCUMULATION. A rung dies, but late (blk >= 4) and at a different
#   block index on repeat runs. Then no fixed threshold holds and the
#   killer integrates something with little or no decay. Re-run that same
#   rung with hold=150 and with hold=30: if time-to-death is constant in
#   SECONDS regardless of hold, it is an accumulator; the duty= column
#   gives its fill rate.
#
# METHOD INVALID. `got=` in the SLEEPCAL line does not match `asked=`, or
#   the measured block time is far below pred_blk=. Then sleep() is not
#   honouring sub-second requests, or the engine does not block a script
#   to its tick rate at all, and the constructed duty is not the duty
#   that ran. Report and stop - if it is the second one, that alone
#   overturns the timing model behind every number in field_notes.txt.
#
# NON-MONOTONE. Deaths do not order cleanly across the ladder on repeat
#   runs (82 dies, 90 survives, 95 dies). No sustained-level model holds
#   at all, and this feeds straight back into the central run-to-run
#   variance mystery (+40.7/+111.0/+40.3/+65.5/+64.3s) as its first
#   controlled reproduction.
#
# =====================================================================
# READING THE OUTPUT
# =====================================================================
# SLEEPCAL n=8 asked=<x>s got=<y>s per=<z>s
#   Validity gate, printed BEFORE the plateau. z must equal S. If the
#   engine floors small sleeps to a frame or to 0.1s, z > S and the rung
#   ran at a LOWER duty than its name - the rung is mislabelled, not
#   void; use the duty= column as its real level.
#   CAVEAT: if get_unix_time() turns out to be whole seconds only, this
#   line is useless at high rungs (small S). Then fall back to comparing
#   the blk t= spacing against the header's pred_blk=.
#
# blk N t=<s> hi=<cpu> lo=<cpu> duty=<pct> a=<units>
#   N    block index - FIRST on the line so a truncated final line still
#        localises the death.
#   t    seconds since the plateau began. Spacing between consecutive
#        t values must match pred_blk= from the header.
#   hi   get_cpu() read IMMEDIATELY after 10 unrolled units - the PEAK of
#        the ripple.
#   lo   get_cpu() read IMMEDIATELY after the sleep - the TROUGH.
#        hi/lo together are the first envelope measurement in this
#        project; every previous CPU number ever recorded was a trough.
#   duty MEASURED, not requested: 100 - 100*(4*N*S)/t, computed by the
#        script from its own elapsed clock. This is the number that goes
#        in the write-up. If it drifts from the requested level, believe
#        this column.
#   a    integrity counter, must be exactly 40*N. Anything else means the
#        cell did not execute the line count the source shows and that
#        run's duty is not trustworthy.
#
# SURVIVED lvl=.. blk=.. t=..s a=.. cpu=..
#   Printed ONLY if the rung lived out the full hold. Its ABSENCE is
#   unambiguous proof of death rather than a quiet exit.
#
# IMPORTANT - DO NOT VOID A RUNG BECAUSE hi READS 100.
#   Under the sliding-window model above, a 10-unit burst is exactly one
#   full second of ticks, so hi is EXPECTED to read at or near 100 at
#   every rung, even at lvl=50. That does not mean the plateau failed.
#   The duty cycle is what this test manipulates and the duty= column is
#   what measures it; hi/lo only describe what the METER does with that
#   duty. (The original draft of this test called a rung void if the
#   meter pinned at 100 - that would have thrown away the real result.)
#
# =====================================================================
# OBSERVER EFFECT
# =====================================================================
# * get_cpu() appears EXACTLY twice per block and never in the cell, never
#   in a loop condition, never in the control path. The level is set by
#   sleep() arithmetic alone.
# * The per-block instrument (2 reads, 2 assignments, 1 clock read, 1
#   guard, 1 log) is byte-identical across every rung, and it is already
#   folded into the busy=14*spu figure the sleep is sized against, so it
#   does not bias the duty it is measuring - and it cancels exactly in
#   every cross-rung comparison, which is the only comparison made here.
# * The duty= column is derived from the wall clock and the sleep total,
#   so whatever the instrument costs is counted as busy time
#   automatically. A rung's true level is measured, never assumed.
# * The paced and control arms are separate loops so the control is
#   genuinely unpaced - no `if S > 0` branch inside the hot cell adding a
#   tick the ladder does not have.
#
# =====================================================================
# HOW TO RUN  (@param binds POSITIONALLY - no -flag= syntax)
#   test_kill_plateau <lvl> <tier> <hold>
# tier = ticks/sec from T02 (10 base, 20 Mk.II, 40 Mk.III, 80 Mk.IV,
# 160 Mk.V). If T02 has not been run, leave it at 10 and say so.
#
# MINIMUM VIABLE PASS - 3 runs, about 4 minutes, answers the headline:
#   1) ps / jobs, confirm nothing else running, paste that too.
#   2) test_kill_plateau 100      <- positive control. MUST die.
#   3) test_kill_plateau 50       <- safest rung.
#   4) test_kill_plateau 95       <- hardest rung short of the ceiling.
#   If 100 dies and both 50 and 95 survive, the threshold is the ceiling
#   and the ladder below is unnecessary.
#
# FULL LADDER - only if 95 or 50 dies. Bisect, one run per rung:
#   test_kill_plateau 82 , then 70 / 90 as the result directs, then 60.
#   Then 3 runs each at the highest surviving rung and the lowest fatal
#   rung. The point of the 3 repeats is NOT precision - it is to tell a
#   sharp deterministic threshold from a stochastic one. A rung that
#   survives twice and dies once is a far more important result than any
#   particular threshold value, and would be the first controlled
#   reproduction of the run-to-run variance this whole programme exists
#   for.
#
# PASTE BACK every line, including the header lines and the
# "[SYSTEM] CPU overloaded..." banner if it appears. A paste that ends
# mid-ladder is still usable - every block line stands on its own.
#
# RUNTIME: ~70-95 seconds per run at base tier (60s plateau + ~8s
# preamble + the sleep calibration + at most one block of overshoot).
# The control arm ends in ~10 seconds if it behaves.
#
# @param lvl "target sustained load percent: 100 (control) 95 90 82 70 60 50" default="82"
# @param tier "CPU ticks per second from T02: 10 20 40 80 160" default="10"
# @param hold "seconds to hold the plateau" default="60"

var LVL = get_param("lvl", "82")
var TIER = get_param("tier", "10")
var HOLD = get_param("hold", "60")
sleep(1)
var L = int(LVL)
var RATE = int(TIER)
var H = int(HOLD)
var SPU = 1.0 / RATE
sleep(1)
var BUSY = 14.0 * SPU
var S = BUSY * (100 - L) / L
var a = 0
var b = 0
var c = 0
sleep(1)
var hi = 0
var lo = 0
var el = 1
var t0 = 0
var ts = 0
var te = 0
sleep(2)
log("PLATEAU lvl=" + LVL + " tier=" + TIER + " hold=" + HOLD + "s spu=" + str(SPU) + " busy_per_cell=" + str(BUSY) + "s S=" + str(S) + "s")
sleep(1)
log("PLATEAU pred_cell=" + str(BUSY + S) + "s pred_blk=" + str(4.0 * (BUSY + S)) + "s entry_cpu=" + str(get_cpu()))
sleep(3)

if S > 0:
    ts = get_unix_time()
    sleep(S)
    sleep(S)
    sleep(S)
    sleep(S)
    sleep(S)
    sleep(S)
    sleep(S)
    sleep(S)
    te = get_unix_time()
    log("SLEEPCAL n=8 asked=" + str(8.0 * S) + "s got=" + str(te - ts) + "s per=" + str((te - ts) / 8.0) + "s (per must equal S)")
    sleep(2)
    log("PLATEAU-RUN lvl=" + LVL + " PACED begin - blk = 4 cells = 40 units + 4 sleeps")
    t0 = get_unix_time()
    while get_unix_time() - t0 < H and b < 60:
        c = 0
        while c < 3:
            c = c + 1
            a = a + 1
            a = a + 1
            a = a + 1
            a = a + 1
            a = a + 1
            a = a + 1
            a = a + 1
            a = a + 1
            a = a + 1
            a = a + 1
            sleep(S)
        a = a + 1
        a = a + 1
        a = a + 1
        a = a + 1
        a = a + 1
        a = a + 1
        a = a + 1
        a = a + 1
        a = a + 1
        a = a + 1
        hi = get_cpu()
        sleep(S)
        lo = get_cpu()
        b = b + 1
        el = get_unix_time() - t0
        if el < 1:
            el = 1
        log("blk " + str(b) + " t=" + str(el) + " hi=" + str(hi) + " lo=" + str(lo) + " duty=" + str(round(10.0 * (100.0 - 400.0 * b * S / el)) / 10.0) + " a=" + str(a))
    log("SURVIVED lvl=" + LVL + " blk=" + str(b) + " t=" + str(get_unix_time() - t0) + "s a=" + str(a) + " cpu=" + str(get_cpu()))
else:
    log("PLATEAU-RUN lvl=" + LVL + " CONTROL begin - no sleep in the cell, duty=100 by construction, MUST die in blk 1-2")
    t0 = get_unix_time()
    while get_unix_time() - t0 < H and b < 60:
        c = 0
        while c < 3:
            c = c + 1
            a = a + 1
            a = a + 1
            a = a + 1
            a = a + 1
            a = a + 1
            a = a + 1
            a = a + 1
            a = a + 1
            a = a + 1
            a = a + 1
        a = a + 1
        a = a + 1
        a = a + 1
        a = a + 1
        a = a + 1
        a = a + 1
        a = a + 1
        a = a + 1
        a = a + 1
        a = a + 1
        hi = get_cpu()
        lo = get_cpu()
        b = b + 1
        el = get_unix_time() - t0
        log("blk " + str(b) + " t=" + str(el) + " hi=" + str(hi) + " lo=" + str(lo) + " duty=100 a=" + str(a))
    log("SURVIVED-CONTROL lvl=" + LVL + " blk=" + str(b) + " t=" + str(get_unix_time() - t0) + "s a=" + str(a) + " cpu=" + str(get_cpu()) + " <<< UNPACED BURN DID NOT DIE - LADDER RESULTS ARE VOID, REPORT THIS")
